{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Let there be darkness.</b> And it would grow dark upon them with darkness beyond the darkness of night — the darkness of night would depart and it would grow dark yet more." "<b>Let there be darkness.</b> Like וְיַאֲמֵשׁ; we have many words missing alef — since the pronunciation of the alef is not so discernible, Scripture is not particular about its absence — as in ״וְלֹא יַהֵל שָׁם עֲרָבִי״ (Isaiah 13), like לֹא יַאֲהֵל — he would not pitch his tent; and likewise ״וַתַּזְרֵנִי חַיִל״ (II Samuel 22), like וַתְּאַזְּרֵנִי. And Onkelos translated it in the sense of removal, like לֹא יָמוּשׁ: ״בָּתַר דְּיֶעְדֵּי קְבַל לֵילְיָא״ — when it would arrive near the light of day. But the comment does not settle on the vav of וְיָמֵשׁ, because it is written after וִיהִי חֹשֶׁךְ. And another master interprets it in the sense of ״מְמַשֵּׁשׁ בַּצָּהֳרָיִם״ (Deuteronomy 28) — that it was doubled and redoubled and thick, until there was substance to it."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:51:27.851122751Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}